+# Agent Note: Session-local whole-turn routing
+
+Status: implemented
+
+English | [中文](2026-11-20-session-local-turn-routing.zh.md)
+
+## Problem
+
+A provider that executes a full turn cannot fit inside the ordinary model adapter: its process owns context and tools, while Harness owns the visible Session and its queue. A separate conversation view would split user history and make switching providers change the Session identity. Reusing the ordinary provider catalog would instead expose a Session-only route in deployment defaults and delegated work.
+
+## Decision
+
+[Agent Loop](../../../../packages/core/agent-loop/README.md#turn-executor) owns the current turn and its durable input and outcome. [Session Controller](../../../../packages/api/session-controller/README.md#whole-turn-providers) registers the interactive admission separately. Model selection changes the next route. A caller still needs permission for the current executor to stop its work, and for the route that originally accepted a prompt to acknowledge its retry. Queue occurrences retain their authorization only for the life of the registration that accepted them. A model selection or the request's own description of its source does not supply caller authority; this continues the [Peer principal decision](2026-10-12-peer-principal-and-remote-access.md).
+
+The composer and `/model` read the same conversation-scoped catalog and use the same selection operation. The composer carries the known selection across asynchronous preparation. The follow request opts into whole-turn activity separately from Assistant streaming so previously deployed strict stream readers do not receive a new frame merely for requesting Assistant streaming.
+
+## Alternatives considered
+
+**Separate external conversations.** This keeps provider history separate but splits the user's Workspace and Session operations. Keeping one Session preserves the native history, search, and queue. Restricting automatic reads of mixed history is the cost of retaining the text there.
+
+**Ordinary LLM adapter.** This would send Harness prompt assembly and tools through a request that already has its own tool loop. It would also make switching providers halfway through an ordinary turn ambiguous.
+
+## Consequences
+
+Whole-turn routes require both registrations and an exact grant before dispatch. The provider is responsible for checking its external configuration and resolving uncertain delivery without replay. A mixed Session remains readable as ordinary history; stock auxiliary title generation, compaction, and raw-log upload omit it. Explicit ordinary prompts can still include retained text in their regular context. This permits same-Session continuation and deliberately gives up automatic recovery of external work after process loss.
